---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SERVICE_BRANCHES } from "../../config/services";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Services — ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description="Training in self-directed learning, and agentic harness automation for a team or a single engineer."
  section="services"
>
  <p class="section-label">Services</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">Two kinds of leverage, offered as work.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The blog covers how this is built. These are the same two threads as engagements: the method
    for teaching yourself a skill, and the harness that makes a coding agent worth handing work to.
  </p>

  <ul class="service-grid">
    {SERVICE_BRANCHES.map((branch) => (
      <li class="service-card">
        <p class="service-card__kicker">{branch.label}</p>
        <h2>{branch.tagline}</h2>
        <p>{branch.description}</p>
        <ul class="service-card__list">
          {branch.offerings.map((offering) => (
            <li>{offering.title}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <a class="read-more" href={`/services/${branch.slug}/`}>
          {branch.label} details &rarr;
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <section class="fit-panel" aria-labelledby="fit-heading">
    <p class="section-label">Good fit</p>
    <h2 id="fit-heading">Bring a repeated problem, not a request for magic.</h2>
    <div class="fit-panel__grid">
      <div>
        <h3>Useful starting points</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>You have a workflow that repeats often enough to measure.</li>
          <li>You want a working first slice, not a slide deck about automation.</li>
          <li>You are willing to review the result and keep the method in-house.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Probably not the right fit</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>You need staff augmentation or an always-on support desk.</li>
          <li>You want a generic course with no connection to your actual work.</li>
          <li>You are not ready to make time for a small experiment.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="process-panel">
    <p class="section-label">How an engagement works</p>
    <ol class="service-process">
      <li>
        <strong>Start with the problem.</strong>
        <span>We identify the repeated work, the constraint that matters, and what a useful first result looks like.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Build a small working version.</strong>
        <span>The first pass stays narrow enough to use and review, rather than becoming a large automation project before it has evidence.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leave you with the method.</strong>
        <span>You get the workflow, decisions, and next steps in a form you can keep using after the engagement ends.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-panel">
    <h2>Enquiring about an engagement</h2>
    <p>
      This runs alongside a full-time job, so I keep a hard cap of two concurrent engagements and
      review new enquiries in batches rather than as they arrive. The waitlist is how that queue
      works.
    </p>
    <a class="cta-panel__action" href="/waitlist/">Join the waitlist &rarr;</a>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
